  <header class="masthead">
    <h1 class="title" style="font-size:34px;">About the Book</h1>
    <p class="strapline"><em>The Performance of Obedience: Legitimacy, Law, and the Systems We Pretend to Believe In</em>, by Greg Lewis.</p>
  </header>
<nav class="toc">
  <a href="{{ '/chapters/' | url }}">Browse by chapter</a>
  <a href="{{ '/search/' | url }}">Search by keyword</a>
  <a href="{{ '/about/' | url }}">About this record</a>
</nav>
  <main>
    <p style="font-size:18px;text-align:justify;">The book starts from an ordinary experience: noticing that many rules are not applied as written, and that almost everyone, including the people responsible for enforcing them, knows it. Its argument is that this unequal application is not a series of exceptions or lapses in implementation, but one of the ordinary ways modern institutions work. Seen that way, much of what is usually explained as failure begins to look structural rather than accidental.</p>
    <p style="font-size:18px;text-align:justify;">It opens with a single example. Diplomatic missions in London owe more than &pound;164 million in unpaid Congestion Charge, while an ordinary motorist who ignores the same charge faces penalties, debt registration and enforcement agents. From there the book moves through law, policing, education, work, markets, welfare and politics, tracing the same pattern in each. It is not an academic study, a manifesto or a catalogue of scandal; it is written from direct experience, and from paying close attention to how rules behave once they are applied to real people.</p>
    <p style="font-size:18px;text-align:justify;">No conspiracy is needed for any of this. The book shows how uneven enforcement becomes the rational choice for the people operating each system, and how official language then keeps the arrangement looking fair. Its underlying subject is legitimacy: people can go on complying with institutions long after they have stopped believing in them, and the later chapters examine what happens when they do.</p>

    <h2 style="font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:16px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.06em;color:var(--ink);margin:44px 0 24px;text-align:left;">How the book is structured</h2>

    <div class="chapter-group">
      <h2>Part I: When the Law Stops Meaning What It Says</h2>
      <p style="font-size:17px;text-align:justify;">Begins with familiar rules, such as speed limits, that are presented as absolute but enforced as flexible, so that people learn to adjust to the practical limit rather than the written one. It then shows how selective enforcement stops being the exception and becomes the normal mode of operation, with consequences decided by visibility and exposure rather than by conduct.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Chapter 1: The Illusion of Law</li>
        <li>Chapter 2: Selective Enforcement and Threshold Logic</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter-group">
      <h2>Part II: Learning the System</h2>
      <p style="font-size:17px;text-align:justify;">Looks at how people come to understand the system in practice: the unwritten lessons of school, work organised around visible activity rather than useful outcomes, markets treated as neutral when they are not, professions whose work mainly justifies one another, and school results that take no account of who was admitted in the first place.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Chapter 3: Learning the Rules That Are Never Taught</li>
        <li>Chapter 4: The Performance of Work</li>
        <li>Chapter 5: Markets Without Meaning</li>
        <li>Chapter 6: The Professional Loop</li>
        <li>Chapter 7: The Advantage Nobody Measured</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter-group">
      <h2>Part III: Moral Inversion</h2>
      <p style="font-size:17px;text-align:justify;">Examines how moral judgement is turned around: employment treated as proof of character and poverty as personal failure, homelessness contained rather than resolved, hunger presented as a shortage of food while food is discarded nearby, and some harmful activities prohibited while others are licensed, advertised and taxed.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Chapter 8: Work as Virtue, Poverty as Failure</li>
        <li>Chapter 9: Managing Failure Instead of Resolving It</li>
        <li>Chapter 10: The Big Lie</li>
        <li>Chapter 11: The Managed Vice</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter-group">
      <h2>Part IV: Criminality and Control</h2>
      <p style="font-size:17px;text-align:justify;">Turns to drug policy and policing, where the formal rules depart most visibly from practice. Whether the same behaviour is ignored or prosecuted depends on who is involved and where, and police discretion becomes the means by which uneven treatment settles into routine, producing consistent patterns without anyone needing to coordinate them.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Chapter 12: Illegal, Illicit, and Ignored</li>
        <li>Chapter 13: Discretion as Power</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter-group">
      <h2>Part V: Withdrawal</h2>
      <p style="font-size:17px;text-align:justify;">Considers what follows: political language that stands in for action, disengagement understood as a response to experience rather than apathy, the quiet ways people step back from institutions while still complying with them, and administrative systems that increasingly judge people by behavioural patterns rather than by their circumstances.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Chapter 14: Symbolic Politics and the Maintenance of Appearances</li>
        <li>Chapter 15: Disengagement Is Not Apathy</li>
        <li>Chapter 16: Opting Out</li>
        <li>Chapter 17: Behaviour Without Direct Surveillance</li>
        <li>Chapter 18: The End of Plausible Deniability</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="font-size:18px;text-align:justify;">Parts II to V each close with empirical notes setting out the published data behind the argument, and the book ends with an epilogue, What Legitimacy Requires.</p>

    <h2 style="font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:16px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.06em;color:var(--ink);margin:44px 0 24px;text-align:left;">The book and this site</h2>
    <p style="font-size:18px;text-align:justify;">Every entry on Citizen Knowledge is mapped to one of the chapters above, and <a href="{{ '/chapters/' | url }}">Browse by Chapter</a> lists the entries in the same order. <em>The Performance of Obedience</em> is currently unpublished and with UK literary agents. The manuscript is still being revised, so the structure shown here reflects its current version and may change before publication; the <a href="{{ '/about/' | url }}#manuscript-note">note on the manuscript</a> explains how this affects earlier entries.</p>
    <p style="font-size:18px;text-align:justify;">Greg Lewis's working life has ranged from senior positions in large international businesses to periods without stable housing, and the book draws on both. This is his first book.</p>
